A hidden, experimental `--recursive-modules` option, requiring `--member-invariants`, begins recursion across module boundaries (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/466). With it, the modules of a cycle of plain imports form a recursive module group, which means what one module with the declarations of all its members would mean: the members' types, constants, functions, methods and lemmas may use each other, a call from one member to another is checked for termination as a call inside one module is, the rules about the declarations of a module (cycles among constants, type synonyms and constraints, datatypes with no value or left of an arrow, equality support) apply to the group, and export sets hide from the other members what they hide from any importer. A cycle that goes through a `refines` or `replaces` clause, an abstract import, the binding of an inherited abstract import, or the nesting of a module in another is an error that names that edge; a replaceable module may not be in a group; and a program that has a group may not declare a codatatype. Not yet supported, and an error: a member that is abstract, refines or replaces a module, or declares an abstract import or an invariant; a type that extends a trait of another member; a module outside a group that refines a member or names one in an abstract import; a group in a library; and compiling a program that has a group. `--show-hints` lists the members of each group. A `.doo` library built with the option requires it of its clients; one built without it is unchanged. Without the option, every diagnostic is unchanged.
